\chapter{The Numeric Core in C\#}\label{ch:win-core}

The Windows port of LineScope is written in C\# for .NET~9, with a WPF user interface. Like the
macOS program it is split in two, and this chapter covers the first half: [[LineScope.Core]], a
class library with no user interface that ports the Swift package [[LineScopeCore]] file by
file. [[PixelBuffer.swift]] becomes [[PixelBuffer.cs]], [[Resampling.swift]] becomes
[[Resampling.cs]], and so on, and the types keep their names in PascalCase.

The mathematics is not repeated here. It was derived in the chapters on pixels
(Chapter~\ref{ch:pixels}), resampling (Chapter~\ref{ch:resampling}), filters
(Chapter~\ref{ch:filters}), analysis (Chapter~\ref{ch:analysis}) and test patterns
(Chapter~\ref{ch:patterns}), and it holds unchanged. This chapter concentrates on the three places
where the port had to differ, because the macOS frameworks it relied on don't exist on Windows:
\begin{itemize}
\item the bridge to 8-bit images, which is premultiplied BGRA instead of Core Graphics' RGBA
  (Section~\ref{sec:win-bgra});
\item the filters that Core Image supplied, now written out on the CPU
  (Section~\ref{sec:win-filters});
\item the FFT that vDSP supplied, now a hand-written radix-2 transform (Section~\ref{sec:win-fft}).
\end{itemize}
Everywhere else the rule is the opposite: the port is kept \emph{identical} to the Swift code.
The resampling kernels and weights, the line sampler, the color conversions, the normalization of
the spectrum, the pattern formulas and the SplitMix64 generator compute the same numbers, so a
profile or a spectrum on Windows matches the one on macOS to floating-point precision. Where C\#
and Swift differ in some small default (rounding, remainders, integer overflow), the C\# code
says explicitly what the Swift code meant. Those spots are pointed out as they come.

\section{The project file}\label{sec:win-core-project}

The library is an SDK-style project. It targets plain [[net9.0]], not [[net9.0-windows]]: nothing
in the core touches WPF or the Windows Imaging Component, so it builds and its tests run on any
platform .NET supports. All the Windows-specific code lives in the app.

<<Windows/LineScope.Core/LineScope.Core.csproj>>=
