\section{Pixels}\label{sec:win-pixels}

The representation is the one of Chapter~\ref{ch:pixels}: interleaved RGBA, 32-bit floats in
$[0,1]$, row 0 at the top, sRGB-encoded values with straight alpha. Only the type that holds it
changes.

<<Windows/LineScope.Core/PixelBuffer.cs>>=
namespace LineScope.Core;

<<Win PixelBuffer: the color sample [[Rgba]]>>

<<Win PixelBuffer: the class>>
@ A color sample is a [[readonly record struct]]. The positional syntax declares the four
components, a constructor with an optional alpha, and value equality in one line, which is what the
Swift struct [[RGBA]] spelled out by hand.

<<Win PixelBuffer: the color sample [[Rgba]]>>=
/// <summary>A color sample with straight (non-premultiplied) alpha, sRGB-encoded components in 0…1.</summary>
public readonly record struct Rgba(float R, float G, float B, float A = 1);
@ \subsection{A class treated as a value}

In Swift, [[PixelBuffer]] is a struct over a copy-on-write array: passing it around is free, and
mutating a copy never affects the original. C\# has nothing like copy-on-write. A struct holding a
[[float]] array would copy only the reference to the array, so two ``copies'' would share their pixels
and the value semantics would be an illusion. The port therefore makes the buffer a
[[sealed class]] and adopts a convention instead: \emph{treat instances as values}. Every
operation returns a new buffer and leaves its input alone; code that wants to change pixels first
calls [[Clone]]. The session can then hand the same buffer to a background task and to a view
without locks, exactly as with the Swift struct.

<<Win PixelBuffer: the class>>=
/// <summary>
/// Interleaved RGBA Float32 image. Row 0 is the top row.
/// Components are sRGB-encoded (not linearized) with straight alpha, so values match what is displayed.
/// Treat instances as values: operations return new buffers instead of mutating their input.
/// </summary>
public sealed class PixelBuffer : IEquatable<PixelBuffer>
{
