@ The designated constructor checks the same invariant as the Swift [[precondition]], but throws an
[[ArgumentException]], the .NET way to reject bad arguments. The filling constructor chains to it,
and chaining has a trap: the array allocated in the argument list is evaluated \emph{before} the validating
constructor runs, and a negative length would throw an [[OverflowException]] with a less helpful
message. Clamping the dimensions to zero first lets the size check report the error. A second
constructor without a color stands in for Swift's default argument, since a default parameter
value in C\# must be a compile-time constant and a [[Rgba]] isn't one.

<<Win PixelBuffer: properties and constructors>>=
public int Width { get; }
public int Height { get; }
public float[] Data { get; }

public PixelBuffer(int width, int height, float[] data)
{
    if (width <= 0 || height <= 0 || data.Length != width * height * 4)
        throw new ArgumentException("Invalid pixel buffer dimensions.");
    Width = width;
    Height = height;
    Data = data;
}

public PixelBuffer(int width, int height) : this(width, height, new Rgba(0, 0, 0, 1)) { }

public PixelBuffer(int width, int height, Rgba fill)
    : this(width, height, new float[Math.Max(width, 0) * Math.Max(height, 0) * 4])
{
    for (int i = 0; i < Data.Length; i += 4)
    {
        Data[i] = fill.R;
        Data[i + 1] = fill.G;
        Data[i + 2] = fill.B;
        Data[i + 3] = fill.A;
    }
}
@ The Swift two-argument subscript becomes an indexer with the same index formula. The [[Data]]
property has no setter, but the array it returns is mutable; that is what lets the indexer's
setter, and the hot loops elsewhere, write into it. C\# arrays are bounds-checked like Swift's, but
the JIT compiler removes many of the checks in simple loops, so the port indexes [[Data]] directly
where Swift needed unsafe buffer pointers.

<<Win PixelBuffer: pixel indexer>>=
public Rgba this[int x, int y]
{
    get
    {
        int i = (y * Width + x) * 4;
        return new Rgba(Data[i], Data[i + 1], Data[i + 2], Data[i + 3]);
    }
    set
    {
        int i = (y * Width + x) * 4;
        Data[i] = value.R;
        Data[i + 1] = value.G;
        Data[i + 2] = value.B;
        Data[i + 3] = value.A;
    }
}
@ Premultiplication is Section~\ref{sec:premultiply} line for line, with [[Math.Clamp]] for
[[min(max(...))]]. One difference is deliberate. Swift's [[fromPremultiplied]] copies its argument
before converting (the copy is free until written, and then it's a real copy). The C\# version
converts \emph{in place} and wraps the same array in the new buffer, saving one image-sized
allocation per filter or resampling. This is safe because every caller passes an array it has
just created and never touches again; the method is [[internal]], so no one else can call it.

<<Win PixelBuffer: premultiplication>>=
// Premultiplication (used by resampling and the blur filters, which work on premultiplied data)

internal float[] Premultiplied()
{
    var output = (float[])Data.Clone();
    for (int i = 0; i < output.Length; i += 4)
    {
        float a = output[i + 3];
        output[i] *= a;
        output[i + 1] *= a;
        output[i + 2] *= a;
    }
    return output;
}

/// <summary>Un-premultiplies (in place) and clamps to 0…1.</summary>
internal static PixelBuffer FromPremultiplied(int width, int height, float[] p)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < p.Length; i += 4)
    {
        float a = Math.Clamp(p[i + 3], 0, 1);
        p[i + 3] = a;
        if (a > 1e-6f)
        {
            p[i] = Math.Clamp(p[i] / a, 0, 1);
            p[i + 1] = Math.Clamp(p[i + 1] / a, 0, 1);
            p[i + 2] = Math.Clamp(p[i + 2] / a, 0, 1);
        }
        else
        {
            p[i] = 0;
            p[i + 1] = 0;
            p[i + 2] = 0;
        }
    }
    return new PixelBuffer(width, height, p);
}
@ \subsection{The 8-bit bridge}\label{sec:win-bgra}

On macOS the buffer converts itself to and from a [[CGImage]], and Core Graphics does the
decoding and color conversion. On Windows those jobs belong to the Windows Imaging Component
(WIC) and to WPF, and both live in the app. The core only exchanges \emph{bytes} with them, which
keeps it free of any UI dependency.

The byte layout is the one WPF draws natively: [[Pbgra32]], four bytes per pixel in the order
blue, green, red, alpha, with the colors premultiplied by alpha. (Read as a little-endian 32-bit
integer, that is ARGB, the traditional Windows pixel.) The app asks WIC to convert every decoded
image to this format, whatever its source layout, just as the Swift decoder draws every
[[CGImage]] into an 8-bit premultiplied bitmap; and it wraps the bytes of a rendered buffer into a
bitmap in this format without further conversion. Compared with the Swift code, only the order of
the color bytes differs: red is at offset 2 and blue at offset 0.

Decoding scales the bytes to $[0,1]$ and un-premultiplies, which is again where 16-bit sources
lose their extra precision.

<<Win PixelBuffer: premultiplied BGRA bridge>>=
// 8-bit bridging (the Windows counterpart of the Core Graphics bridge). The byte layout is
// premultiplied BGRA, which is what WIC's and WPF's Pbgra32 pixel format expects.

/// <summary>Converts decoded 8-bit premultiplied BGRA pixels to a float buffer.</summary>
public static PixelBuffer FromPremultipliedBgra8(int width, int height, byte[] bytes)
{
    if (bytes.Length < width * height * 4) throw new ArgumentException("Too few bytes.");
    var p = new float[width * height * 4];
    for (int i = 0; i < p.Length; i += 4)
    {
        p[i] = bytes[i + 2] / 255f;
        p[i + 1] = bytes[i + 1] / 255f;
        p[i + 2] = bytes[i] / 255f;
        p[i + 3] = bytes[i + 3] / 255f;
    }
    return FromPremultiplied(width, height, p);
}

/// <summary>Renders the buffer as 8-bit premultiplied BGRA for display or export.</summary>
public byte[] ToPremultipliedBgra8()
{
    var bytes = new byte[Data.Length];
    for (int i = 0; i < Data.Length; i += 4)
    {
        float a = Math.Clamp(Data[i + 3], 0, 1);
        bytes[i + 2] = ToByte(Math.Clamp(Data[i], 0, 1) * a);
        bytes[i + 1] = ToByte(Math.Clamp(Data[i + 1], 0, 1) * a);
        bytes[i] = ToByte(Math.Clamp(Data[i + 2], 0, 1) * a);
        bytes[i + 3] = ToByte(a);
    }
    return bytes;
}

<<Win PixelBuffer: rounding to a byte>>
@ Rounding to a byte is the first of the small defaults that differ between the languages. Swift's
[[rounded()]] rounds halves away from zero; .NET's [[Math.Round]] rounds halves to the nearest
\emph{even} integer unless told otherwise. A value of $126.5$ would become $127$ on macOS and
$126$ on Windows. Asking for [[MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero]] makes the exported bytes agree.

<<Win PixelBuffer: rounding to a byte>>=
private static byte ToByte(float v) => (byte)MathF.Round(v * 255, MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero);
@ Swift synthesizes [[Equatable]] for the struct. A class compares by reference unless told
otherwise, so the port implements [[IEquatable]] by hand: same size and the same floats, compared
as spans. The hash code combines only the dimensions. That is cheap and consistent with
[[Equals]] (equal buffers have equal sizes), and buffers are compared in tests, not used as
dictionary keys.

<<Win PixelBuffer: value equality>>=
public bool Equals(PixelBuffer? other) =>
    other is not null && Width == other.Width && Height == other.Height && Data.AsSpan().SequenceEqual(other.Data);

public override bool Equals(object? obj) => Equals(obj as PixelBuffer);

public override int GetHashCode() => HashCode.Combine(Width, Height, Data.Length);
@

\section{Resampling}\label{sec:win-resampling}

The resampler is the part of the port that must agree with macOS most closely, since comparing
resampling methods is what LineScope is for. Kernels, supports, pixel-center convention, trimming
and normalization are therefore exactly those of Chapter~\ref{ch:resampling}. The one real change
is that both passes now run in parallel.

<<Windows/LineScope.Core/Resampling.cs>>=
