@ [[Number]] reproduces [[.precision(.fractionLength(0...3))]] with a custom format string. In
[[#,0.###]], the comma asks for digit grouping, [[0]] forces at least one integer digit, and each
[[#]] after the point is an optional decimal, so trailing zeros vanish: $2$ becomes ``2'' and
$0.125$ becomes ``0.125''. With no decimals allowed the format is just [[#,0]].

The culture is passed explicitly. [[CultureInfo.CurrentCulture]] follows the regional settings of
Windows, so with a Spanish locale a tab reads ``Gaussian r=0,5'' and a large size ``1.024'', as
the same user would expect from any other program; macOS's formatter behaves the same way. Passing
the culture, instead of relying on the implicit default, also documents that the choice is
intended: the parameter dialog parses what the user types with the same culture (and accepts a
period as well), so what is displayed can be typed back. How the last digit is rounded is left to
each platform's formatter.

