@ The conditioned samples become the real parts of a complex array of length $N$; the rest of the
array, and every imaginary part, is zero. That is the zero padding, and it happens by itself,
because a new array of [[Complex]] is zero-initialized.

Swift's version used vDSP's \emph{packed real} FFT, which transforms the $N$ real samples as
$N/2$ complex numbers and unpacks the result. The port transforms the full complex array instead.
This does about twice the arithmetic, since half of the input is known to be zero, but a profile
has at most a few thousand samples and the transform takes microseconds; the simpler code wins.
[[Complex]] is also double precision, where vDSP worked in [[float]], so the port's transform is
if anything slightly more accurate.

<<Win Spectrum: complex FFT of the padded signal>>=
var buffer = new Complex[fftSize];
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) buffer[i] = signal[i];
Fft.Forward(buffer);
@ Here the two normalizations meet. vDSP's packed output held $2X_k$ for $0 < k < N/2$, and
squeezed the two real bins into one slot, $2X_0$ in the real part and $2X_{N/2}$ in the imaginary
part of element 0; the Swift code therefore unpacked element 0 and divided everything by 2.
The complex FFT returns the plain, unscaled $X_k = \sum_j x_j e^{-2\pi i jk/N}$, with $X_0$ at
index 0 and $X_{N/2}$ at index $N/2$, each in its own place. Both the unpacking and the halving
disappear, and what remains is literally the formula of Chapter~\ref{ch:analysis}:
\[
  A_0 = \frac{|X_0|}{\sum_i w_i},\qquad
  A_k = \frac{2\,|X_k|}{\sum_i w_i}\quad(0<k<N/2),\qquad
  A_{N/2} = \frac{|X_{N/2}|}{\sum_i w_i}.
\]
The bins above $N/2$ are the complex conjugates of those below, because the input is real, and
are ignored.

<<Win Spectrum: single-sided amplitudes>>=
var amplitudes = new double[half + 1];
double norm = Math.Max(windowSum, 1e-12);
amplitudes[0] = buffer[0].Magnitude / norm;
amplitudes[half] = buffer[half].Magnitude / norm;
for (int k = 1; k < Math.Max(half, 1); k++)
    amplitudes[k] = 2 * buffer[k].Magnitude / norm;
