<<Win Spectrum: frequency axes>>=
double lineLength = spacing * (n - 1);
var cpp = new double[half + 1];
var cpl = new double[half + 1];
for (int k = 0; k <= half; k++)
{
    cpp[k] = k / (fftSize * spacing);
    cpl[k] = cpp[k] * lineLength;
}
return new SpectrumResult(cpp, cpl, amplitudes, fftSize, n);
@ \subsection{The FFT}\label{sec:win-fft}

The transform is the iterative radix-2 algorithm of Cooley and Tukey~\cite{cooley65}, in its
decimation-in-time form. Its idea is to split a DFT of length $N$ into the DFTs of the
even-indexed and the odd-indexed samples, $E_k$ and $O_k$, each of length $N/2$:
\[
  X_k = E_k + W_N^k\,O_k,\qquad X_{k+N/2} = E_k - W_N^k\,O_k,\qquad
  W_N = e^{-2\pi i/N},\quad 0 \le k < N/2 .
\]
Applied recursively down to length 1, this costs $O(N\log N)$ operations instead of the $O(N^2)$
of the definition. The iterative version runs the recursion bottom up and in place: first it
reorders the input so that every sub-transform finds its samples next to each other, then it
combines neighbors into transforms of length 2, 4, 8, \dots, $N$.

<<Win Spectrum: the radix-2 FFT>>=
/// <summary>In-place iterative radix-2 Cooley-Tukey FFT (replaces vDSP on macOS).</summary>
internal static class Fft
{
    /// <summary>Forward DFT, X[k] = Σ x[j]·e^(−2πi·jk/N), unscaled. The length must be a power of two.</summary>
    public static void Forward(Complex[] a)
    {
        int n = a.Length;
        if (n <= 1) return;
        if ((n & (n - 1)) != 0) throw new ArgumentException("FFT length must be a power of two.");

        <<Win Fft: bit-reversal permutation>>

        <<Win Fft: butterflies>>
    }
}
@ Splitting into even and odd samples at every level means that, after all the splits, sample $j$
ends up at the position whose binary digits are those of $j$ reversed. For $N = 8$, sample
$1 = 001_2$ goes to position $100_2 = 4$, and sample $3 = 011_2$ to $110_2 = 6$. The loop walks
$i = 1, \dots, N-1$ while keeping $j$ equal to the bit reversal of $i$. Incrementing a reversed
number means adding 1 at its \emph{top} bit and carrying downward: clear the leading ones until
the first zero, then set that zero. The inner [[for]] clears the ones, and the [[j ^= bit]] after
it sets the zero. Each pair is swapped once, when $i < j$; positions with $i = j$ stay put.

<<Win Fft: bit-reversal permutation>>=
// Bit-reversal permutation.
for (int i = 1, j = 0; i < n; i++)
{
    int bit = n >> 1;
    for (; (j & bit) != 0; bit >>= 1) j ^= bit;
    j ^= bit;
    if (i < j) (a[i], a[j]) = (a[j], a[i]);
}
@ Each stage combines pairs of transforms of length $\mathit{len}/2$, lying next to each other,
into one transform of length $\mathit{len}$, by the \emph{butterfly} above: $u$ is $E_k$, $v$ is
$W^k O_k$, and they are replaced by $u+v$ and $u-v$. The twiddle factor $w = W_{\mathit{len}}^k$
starts at 1 for every block and is advanced by one multiplication with
$W_{\mathit{len}} = e^{-2\pi i/\mathit{len}}$ per step, instead of calling [[Math.Cos]] and
[[Math.Sin]] for every $k$. The recurrence accumulates rounding error proportional to the block
length, which in double precision stays far below the float precision of the input for any
length LineScope uses.

The output is unscaled, as the documentation comment states, and that is what the amplitude
normalization above assumes. The tests check the transform against a direct evaluation of the
DFT's definition.

<<Win Fft: butterflies>>=
for (int len = 2; len <= n; len <<= 1)
{
    double angle = -2 * Math.PI / len;
    var wLen = new Complex(Math.Cos(angle), Math.Sin(angle));
    int halfLen = len >> 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i += len)
    {
        Complex w = Complex.One;
        for (int k = 0; k < halfLen; k++)
        {
            Complex u = a[i + k];
            Complex v = a[i + k + halfLen] * w;
            a[i + k] = u + v;
            a[i + k + halfLen] = u - v;
            w *= wLen;
        }
    }
}
@

