@ \subsection{SplitMix64}

The generator is SplitMix64 with the same constants. Swift needs the wrapping operators [[&+=]]
and [[&*]], because its ordinary arithmetic traps on overflow. C\# arithmetic wraps by default,
but a project can turn on overflow checking for the whole assembly, and then the multiplications
would throw. The [[unchecked]] block states that wrapping modulo $2^{64}$ is the intended
arithmetic, whatever the project settings.

The struct uses a primary constructor to take the seed. It is mutable, and [[rng]] above is a
local variable, so each call to [[Next]] advances the one generator rather than a copy.

<<Win TestPattern: SplitMix64>>=
/// <summary>Small deterministic generator so noise patterns are reproducible from their seed.</summary>
internal struct SplitMix64(ulong seed)
{
    private ulong _state = seed;

    public ulong Next()
    {
        unchecked
        {
            _state += 0x9E37_79B9_7F4A_7C15;
            ulong z = _state;
            z = (z ^ (z >> 30)) * 0xBF58_476D_1CE4_E5B9;
            z = (z ^ (z >> 27)) * 0x94D0_49BB_1331_11EB;
            return z ^ (z >> 31);
        }
    }
}
